WEBVTT

1
00:00:11.323 --> 00:00:11.744
When Mr.

2
00:00:11.804 --> 00:00:12.324
Hiram B.

3
00:00:12.404 --> 00:00:16.845
Otis decided to buy the castle in Canterville, everyone told him he was being foolish.

4
00:00:17.565 --> 00:00:19.126
There was no doubt that it was haunted.

5
00:00:19.926 --> 00:00:24.727
In fact, when they met to discuss the sale, Lord Canterville told him that his family had stopped

6
00:00:24.788 --> 00:00:26.088
living there because of the ghost.

7
00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:32.122
Two skeleton hands had grasped his great aunt's shoulders at dinner one night, scaring her out of her

8
00:00:32.162 --> 00:00:39.465
wits. And Lord Canterville's wife, Lady Canterville, was never able to sleep there because of the mysterious noises

9
00:00:39.545 --> 00:00:40.286
in the library.

10
00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:42.587
"My lord," said Mr.

11
00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:46.608
Otis, "my family is thoroughly modern, and we don't believe in ghosts.

12
00:00:47.429 --> 00:00:51.690
Why, if there were such a thing as ghosts, I'm sure someone would have put one in the

13
00:00:51.730 --> 00:00:53.371
public museum a long time ago."

14
00:00:53.391 --> 00:00:57.541
"I'm afraid you are mistaken, sir," said Lord Canterville very calmly.

15
00:00:57.561 --> 00:00:59.882
"I assure you the ghost exists."

16
00:00:59.902 --> 00:01:03.844
"I tell you, there is no such thing," snapped Mr.

17
00:01:03.944 --> 00:01:08.267
Otis. Lord Canterville didn't press the subject, and they concluded the sale.

18
00:01:09.167 --> 00:01:10.428
He felt that the modern Mr.

19
00:01:10.508 --> 00:01:11.929
Otis had been properly warned.

20
00:01:12.829 --> 00:01:18.752
Mr. Otis, for his part, happily bought the castle, "ghost and all," as he said when he shook

21
00:01:18.792 --> 00:01:19.793
Lord Canterville's hand.

22
00:01:20.793 --> 00:01:25.256
A few weeks later, on a lovely July evening with a delicate scent of pinewoods in the air,

23
00:01:26.068 --> 00:01:28.550
the Otis family arrived at the gates of their new castle.

24
00:01:29.491 --> 00:01:32.493
Sitting in the front of the carriage beside her husband was Mrs.

25
00:01:32.573 --> 00:01:38.197
Otis, a handsome, middle-aged woman, with a healthy complexion and modern sensibility.

26
00:01:39.117 --> 00:01:42.920
Her oldest son, Washington, was a fair-haired and eager young man.

27
00:01:43.921 --> 00:01:47.283
Sitting beside Washington in the back of the carriage was his sister Virginia.

28
00:01:48.044 --> 00:01:48.984
Miss Virginia E.

29
00:01:49.124 --> 00:01:53.928
Otis was a beautiful girl of seventeen, with a charming freedom in her large blue eyes.

30
00:01:55.052 --> 00:02:00.616
When the family had first seen the castle on a visit to Canterville that spring, Virginia had challenged

31
00:02:00.796 --> 00:02:03.458
old Lord Bilton to a race around the park on horseback.

32
00:02:04.379 --> 00:02:09.783
The handsome young Duke of Cheshire had noticed her immediately and was glad when he learned that Virginia's

33
00:02:09.823 --> 00:02:11.384
father had bought Canterville Castle.

34
00:02:12.165 --> 00:02:17.088
Seated side by side in the carriage across from Virginia were the young twins, who were attempting to

35
00:02:17.148 --> 00:02:18.169
steal each other's hats.

36
00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:21.712
They were delightful boys, but they were always up to mischief.

37
00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:27.221
As the carriage drove up to the castle, Virginia saw squirrels in the trees that lined the drive

38
00:02:27.562 --> 00:02:30.862
and watched rabbits hop away through the grass with their white tails in the air.

39
00:02:31.863 --> 00:02:36.384
When the carriage pulled up to the great wooden doors of the castle, however, the sky became suddenly

40
00:02:36.484 --> 00:02:37.324
dark and gloomy.

41
00:02:38.385 --> 00:02:41.246
On the castle steps stood an old woman dressed in black silk.

42
00:02:42.466 --> 00:02:43.766
This was the housekeeper, Mrs.

43
00:02:43.846 --> 00:02:49.308
Umney. "I bid you welcome to Canterville Castle," she said in an old-fashioned manner.

44
00:02:50.624 --> 00:02:55.948
They all followed her into the library, a long oak-paneled room with a large stained-glass window and a

45
00:02:55.988 --> 00:02:57.269
great stone fireplace.

46
00:02:58.509 --> 00:03:01.031
Tea was already set out for them on a silver tray.

47
00:03:02.412 --> 00:03:08.136
As the family removed their coats and sat down, Virginia walked over to examine the beautiful stained-glass window.

48
00:03:09.337 --> 00:03:12.779
In the middle pane, an inscription was formed in curious black letters.

49
00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:19.724
When a golden girl can win Prayer from out the lips of sin, When the barren almond bears,

50
00:03:20.192 --> 00:03:25.957
And a little child gives away its tears, Then shall all the house be still And peace come

51
00:03:25.977 --> 00:03:26.677
to Canterville.

52
00:03:27.698 --> 00:03:33.663
Just as she was wondering what the strange poem could mean, she heard her mother's surprised voice: "Oh

53
00:03:33.703 --> 00:03:36.165
my, I'm afraid there's a bad stain on the carpet."

54
00:03:37.046 --> 00:03:40.388
Everyone came to look at the dull red stain just in front of the fireplace.

55
00:03:40.408 --> 00:03:44.892
"Yes, madam," said the old housekeeper, "blood has been spilled on that spot."

56
00:03:45.612 --> 00:03:46.994
"How horrible," said Mrs.

57
00:03:47.074 --> 00:03:49.756
Otis. "I don't think we want bloodstains in our home.

58
00:03:50.048 --> 00:03:51.028
You must clean it up."

59
00:03:52.029 --> 00:03:53.669
Mrs. Umney spoke mysteriously.

60
00:03:53.689 --> 00:03:59.411
"It is the blood of Lady Eleanore of Canterville," she said, "who was murdered on that very spot

61
00:03:59.451 --> 00:04:02.512
by her husband Sir Simon of Canterville three hundred years ago.

62
00:04:03.572 --> 00:04:07.133
Sir Simon disappeared nine years later, and his body was never found.

63
00:04:07.913 --> 00:04:09.854
His ghost haunts the castle to this day.

64
00:04:10.534 --> 00:04:11.934
The stain cannot be removed."

65
00:04:11.954 --> 00:04:15.275
"That's nonsense," said Washington right away.

66
00:04:15.915 --> 00:04:16.476
Before Mrs.

67
00:04:16.536 --> 00:04:21.935
Umney could protest, he pulled out a bottle of Champion Stain Remover and quickly scrubbed away the stain.

68
00:04:21.955 --> 00:04:24.196
"I knew it," he exclaimed.

69
00:04:25.217 --> 00:04:29.240
No sooner had he said these words than a terrible flash of lightning lit up the room.

70
00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:30.541
Everyone's heart leaped.

71
00:04:31.421 --> 00:04:34.083
"I have seen terrible, terrible things in this house," Mrs.

72
00:04:34.183 --> 00:04:36.484
Umney intoned ominously, but Mr.

73
00:04:36.585 --> 00:04:39.446
Otis calmly assured her that no one was afraid of ghosts.

74
00:04:40.447 --> 00:04:43.789
The storm raged fiercely all night, but nothing mysterious occurred.

75
00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:47.652
The next morning, the twins were the first ones down for breakfast.

76
00:04:48.352 --> 00:04:52.953
They were chasing each other around the wing-backed chairs in the library when they stopped in front of

77
00:04:52.993 --> 00:04:53.714
the fireplace.

78
00:04:54.654 --> 00:04:57.335
Sure enough, the terrible bloodstain had returned.

79
00:04:58.275 --> 00:05:03.496
"I don't think it can be the fault of Champion Stain Remover," said Washington when the family gathered

80
00:05:03.516 --> 00:05:04.217
in the library.

81
00:05:04.237 --> 00:05:05.957
"It's always worked before."

82
00:05:06.637 --> 00:05:09.998
He rubbed out the stain again, but the next morning the stain returned.

83
00:05:10.858 --> 00:05:15.740
Mr. Otis locked the library that night before everyone went to bed, but still the stain was there

84
00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:16.280
in the morning.

85
00:05:17.084 --> 00:05:21.447
"It must be the ghost," said Washington as he kneeled to clean up the ghostly mark once again.

86
00:05:22.308 --> 00:05:23.028
That night, Mr.

87
00:05:23.128 --> 00:05:26.571
Otis was awakened by a strange noise in the corridor outside his bedroom.

88
00:05:27.552 --> 00:05:31.714
It sounded like the clanking and squeaking of rusted metal, and it seemed to be coming nearer.

89
00:05:32.595 --> 00:05:35.317
He got up, lit a candle, and put on his slippers.

90
00:05:36.178 --> 00:05:40.361
From his night table he took out a small glass bottle and went to open his bedroom door.

91
00:05:41.181 --> 00:05:44.884
Right in front of him, glowing in the moonlit hallway, was a horrible sight.

92
00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:50.564
A terrible old man stood before him in ancient clothes with eyes burning red.

93
00:05:51.364 --> 00:05:54.707
From his wrists and ankles hung heavy manacles and rusty chains.

94
00:05:54.727 --> 00:05:56.829
"My dear sir," said Mr.

95
00:05:56.909 --> 00:06:00.491
Otis to the ghost, "I must insist that you oil those chains.

96
00:06:01.172 --> 00:06:02.193
They are keeping me awake.

97
00:06:02.853 --> 00:06:05.255
I have brought you a bottle of Rising Sun Lubricator.

98
00:06:05.996 --> 00:06:07.076
It is really quite good."

99
00:06:07.837 --> 00:06:11.420
Mr. Otis placed the bottle on a table in the hallway and returned to bed.

100
00:06:12.456 --> 00:06:17.180
For a moment the Canterville ghost stood perfectly still with his ghastly jaw hanging open.

101
00:06:17.881 --> 00:06:22.324
He knocked the bottle on the floor, uttered a hollow groan, and turned back down the corridor.

102
00:06:23.085 --> 00:06:28.389
When he reached the top of the great oak staircase, a door flew open and two little figures

103
00:06:28.429 --> 00:06:30.551
in matching pajamas tossed their pillows at him.

104
00:06:31.371 --> 00:06:36.516
Fearing further abuse to his dignity, the ghost quickly turned into a green vapor and escaped through the

105
00:06:36.536 --> 00:06:36.796
wall.
